he elements of $X:$\n\n$$\n{1,2,3,4,5},\\,{1,2,3,4},\\,∅\n$$\n\nNote that ${1,2,3,4,5},$ ${1,2,3,4},$ and $∅$ each count as single elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|X|=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181557/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "8985779f-54e0-5594-89f8-6217877a8ca2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186306094 :answer-field/choices ["q-181557/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "32eb2c48-d108-52ed-9271-8252e98acc6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "108c8d4b-ca23-5fc2-843b-dec32eca569b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "1e1ff813-942e-5216-9c68-cd28295a1fec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "2ae26c5f-8915-5625-9445-0025187f80d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "58edd90d-8901-5d02-ab61-cf9eb51d8bbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9848ce-c581-51cf-9be2-e4adc7512f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80735/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-80735/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-80735/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-80735/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-80735/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-80735/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80735" :question/id #uuid "1a913076-e72f-54f0-b9be-9113cfdacd9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80735" :question/problem "If $A={4,{6,11},5,{7,13,15},{8,2}}$, then $|A|=$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n4,\\,{6,11},\\,5,\\,{7,13,15},\\,{8,2}\n$$\n\nNote that ${6,11}$,  ${7,13,15}$ and ${8,2}$ each count as single elements of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|A|=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80735/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80735"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181561/field-1/df73f43b96a18f785e8db1b01e271a36dce622bf9bbecbc440f9f3c6ca0c6e97" :answer/id #uuid "a790c782-9db3-5d30-9e7b-5d2b79eb784c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "179" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181561/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0681fd81-d1a6-552e-9c6f-aa8dcac4d0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181561/field-1/df73f43b96a18f785e8db1b01e271a36dce622bf9bbecbc440f9f3c6ca0c6e97" :answer-field/choices ["q-181561/field-1/df73f43b96a18f785e8db1b01e271a36dce622bf9bbecbc440f9f3c6ca0c6e97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181561" :question/id #uuid "5d67bf8a-a51f-5a68-95c6-b3470ada6ae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181561" :question/problem "If $A={98,102,106,…,810},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n98,\\,102,\\,106,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4 \\\\  & =102-98 \\\\  & =106-102 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =98+(n-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =98+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n+94.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={4n+94:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4N+94 & =810 \\\\ 4N & =716 \\\\ N & =\\frac{716}{4} \\\\ N & =179\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={4n+94:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 179}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $179$ elements, we must have $|A|=179.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181561/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181561"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181566/field-1/dcb0040a5a7b1dd7f5486a7cce8e7779db7d353c08235e7a3f88d2318ff745f4" :answer/id #uuid "ca7fe877-2e4b-5b33-902f-eac0d170d910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1172" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181566/field-1/f3fd1e518440a12809d2b945185bb274837e540cf6b5c1dd0a11c33b18f8c273" :answer/id #uuid "6b939c76-c6b4-54db-a953-2861a40252c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1171" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181566/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e99bfad-f453-5595-88cd-a12a8cd3134a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181566/field-1/dcb0040a5a7b1dd7f5486a7cce8e7779db7d353c08235e7a3f88d2318ff745f4" :answer-field/choices ["q-181566/field-1/dcb0040a5a7b1dd7f5486a7cce8e7779db7d353c08235e7a3f88d2318ff745f4" "q-181566/field-1/f3fd1e518440a12809d2b945185bb274837e540cf6b5c1dd0a11c33b18f8c273"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181566" :question/id #uuid "359ce01e-7589-5ea5-9246-896c5271d8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-181566" :question/problem "If $A={-875,-869,-863,…,6\\,151},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-875,\\,-869,\\,-863,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6 \\\\  & =-869-(-875) \\\\  & =-863-(-869) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-875+(n-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-875+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-881.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={6n-881:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6N-881 & =6\\,151 \\\\ 6N & =7\\,032 \\\\ N & =\\frac{7\\,032}{6} \\\\ N & =1\\,172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={6n-881:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 1\\,172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $1\\,172$ elements, we must have $|A|=1\\,172.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181566/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181566"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181563/field-1/da160edd67963907e8889d92795478f191c3275365178c31a7c6899da9c2cd1a" :answer/id #uuid "0a3e3c04-33bc-5e83-8673-861763310039" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "171" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051856 :answer-field/choices ["q-181563/field-1/da160edd67963907e8889d92795478f191c3275365178c31a7c6899da9c2cd1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181573/field-1/5fbfc5d5f3f35945869d00a90a0fce9b224be03a1a4c6f18eaea220efe57cabd" :answer/id #uuid "6840236d-565f-5bce-b2bc-e90bb8adfff6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2609" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181573/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a39118b0-5fdc-5c1a-b0e5-023f16d9c9b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181573/field-1/5fbfc5d5f3f35945869d00a90a0fce9b224be03a1a4c6f18eaea220efe57cabd" :answer-field/choices ["q-181573/field-1/5fbfc5d5f3f35945869d00a90a0fce9b224be03a1a4c6f18eaea220efe57cabd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181573" :question/id #uuid "b7155e82-f133-5ff9-8973-883ba9513f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-181573" :question/problem "If $A={-615,-610,-605,…,12\\,425},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-615,\\,-610,\\,-605,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-610-(-615) \\\\  & =-605-(-610) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-615+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-615+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-620.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5N-620 & =12\\,425 \\\\ 5N & =13\\,045 \\\\ N & =\\frac{13\\,045}{5} \\\\ N & =2\\,609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 2\\,609}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $2\\,609$ elements, we must have $|A|=2\\,609.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181573/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181573"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181454/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "d61fcce4-b1cc-5f5e-a928-d38d216bb0ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181454/field-1/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "75f89d73-cb7b-5fd4-9d9f-085022d3ab34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051845 :answer-field/correct "q-181454/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-181454/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-181454/field-1/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181454"] :question/problem "If $A={3,6,12,24,48,…,98\\,304},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({2}^{n-1})=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{N} & =98\\,304 \\\\ {2}^{N} & =65\\,536 \\\\ N & ={log}_{2}⁡(65\\,536) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(65\\,536)}{{log}_{10}⁡(2)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181576/field-1/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "05ce2efe-1704-5dad-a131-8dd0858ff065" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186306116 :answer-field/choices ["q-181576/field-1/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181584/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "8fdee566-24a5-5f52-9998-9b048b8c1813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181584/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3718f2c2-13cf-55a6-baf5-30430ca76173" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181584/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer-field/choices ["q-181584/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181584" :question/id #uuid "b4b82edb-27c4-5eb4-8e21-16aeb96703eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-181584" :question/problem "If $A={1,9,81,729,…,3\\,486\\,784\\,401},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,9,\\,81,\\,729,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{9} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {9}^{n-1}={9}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{N-1} & =3\\,486\\,784\\,401 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{9}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401)}{{log}_{10}⁡(9)} \\\\ N-1 & =10 \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181584/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181584"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
